    "problem_statement": "Dict constructor fails with iterable arguments\n\nWhen initializing a Dict with certain iterable arguments like lists of tuples, zip objects, or generators, the constructor raises various errors instead of properly handling the input.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# This should work but raises TypeError\nd1 = Dict(zip(['a', 'b'], [1, 2]))\n\n# This should work but raises TypeError  \nd2 = Dict([('a', 1), ('b', 2)])\n\n# This should work but raises TypeError\nd3 = Dict((x, x*2) for x in range(3))\n\n# This should work but raises IndexError\nd4 = Dict('ab')\n```\n\nThe expected behavior is that these should all create valid Dict objects with the appropriate key-value pairs, similar to how the built-in dict constructor handles these cases."
  },
